-l 0 limits the \ limitation operator on put(values, !&input) to zero successes, so values stays [] regardless of stdin content; repeats.argv's -l 0 + repeats.dat's 3 real lines together confirm SCRIP's \ operator short-circuits storage to zero even with input available to read. The shipped repeats := \opts["2"] | (2 ^ 20) line never reads opts["r"] (options() keys by option name, never "2"), so -r is dead and any non-empty stored line would be written 2^20 times -- empty-by-design is the only bounded, deterministic case this program has.
